<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Kingdoms of Southeast Asia</h1><p class="path-tagline">Follow the trading ports, temple cities and sultanates of mainland and island Southeast Asia.</p><p class="path-intro">Funan, generally considered the first known kingdom of Southeast Asia, traded through its port of Óc Eo with India, China and the Roman world. Along the Mekong, the Vietnamese coast and the straits, later states drew their wealth from sea trade, rice and tribute, and built in stone: the Sailendras raised Borobudur in Java, the Khmer kings Angkor Wat, and the kings of Pagan some 10,000 Buddhist monuments on the Irrawaddy. This path follows these kingdoms in the order of their rise, closing with Ayutthaya on the Chao Phraya and the Muslim sultanate of Malacca, which the Portuguese took in 1511.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Navigator</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/funan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-borei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 50 CE – 627 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Funan</span><span class="path-step-why">Generally considered the first known kingdom of Southeast Asia, Funan traded through its port of Óc Eo, where Roman gold medallions and Indian and Persian goods were found.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/champa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kauthara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 192 CE – 1832 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Champa</span><span class="path-step-why">A confederation of Cham principalities on the coast of present-day Vietnam, whose fleets controlled the spice and silk trade of the South China Sea from the 7th to the 10th century.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/chenla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sambor-prei-kuk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 550 CE – 802 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Chenla</span><span class="path-step-why">The Khmer principalities that broke from Funan absorbed it under Isanavarman I, before Jayavarman II proclaimed the Khmer Empire in 802.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/srivijaya-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 650 CE – 1377 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Srivijaya Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From Palembang in Sumatra this sea power ruled the Strait of Malacca, taxed China-India shipping and became a renowned centre of Buddhist learning.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borobudur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Borobudur</span><span class="path-step-why">One of the largest Buddhist monuments in the world, the temple was built in central Java under the Sailendra dynasty, closely connected to Srivijaya.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/khmer-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angkor-wat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 802 CE – 1431 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Khmer Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded in 802, the empire ruled much of mainland Southeast Asia from Angkor, where vast reservoirs and canals paid for Angkor Wat and Angkor Thom.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/pagan-kingdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ananda-temple.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 849 CE – 1297 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Pagan Kingdom</span><span class="path-step-why">The first state to unite the Irrawaddy valley built some 10,000 Buddhist monuments on the plain of Bagan before Mongol invasions in the 1280s broke it apart.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/majapahit-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trowulan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1293 CE – 1527 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Majapahit Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The high point of Hindu-Buddhist Java claimed suzerainty over much of the archipelago, its fleets paid for by Brantas rice and the spice trade.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-phra-si-sanphet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1351 CE – 1767 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ayutthaya Kingdom</span><span class="path-step-why">The Siamese kingdom on the Chao Phraya absorbed Sukhothai in 1438 and welcomed Chinese, Japanese, Persian and European merchants until Burma destroyed it in 1767.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/malacca-sultanate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parameswara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1400 CE – 1511 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Malacca Sultanate</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded about 1400 by Parameswara under Ming protection, the port became the great entrepôt of the strait and a centre of Islamic learning until the Portuguese took it in 1511.</span></span></a></li></ol>
